n−i , whereP is a palindrome, whose first entry is larger thank. We prove a conjecture due to Gessel, which determines the list that maximizes the descent statistic, among lists of a given size and given length. We also have a generalization of the boustrophedon transform of Millar, Sloane and Young.\n\nImage: ResearchGate Logo\n\nDiscover the world's research\n\n  * 25+ million members\n  * 160+ million publication pages\n  * 2.3+ billion citations\nJoin for free\n\nPublic Full-text 1\n\nContent uploaded by Richard Ehrenborg\n\nAuthor content\n\nAll content in this area was uploaded by Richard Ehrenborg on Oct 25, 2015\n\nContent may be subject to copyright.\n\nMaximizing the descent statistic∗\n\nRichard EHRENBORG and Swapneel MAHAJAN\n\nAbstract\n\nFor a subset S, let the descent statistic β(S) be the number of permutations that have descent\n\nset S. We study inequalities between the descent statistics of subsets. Each subset (and its\n\ncomplement) is encoded by a list containing the lengths of the runs. We deﬁne two preorders that\n\ncompare diﬀerent lists based on the descent statistic. Using these preorders, we obtain a complete\n\norder on lists of the form (k i, P, k n−i), where P is a palindrome, whose ﬁrst entry is larger than k.\n\nWe prove a conjecture due to Gessel, which determines the list that maximizes the descent statistic,\n\namong lists of a given size and given length. We also have a generalization of the boustrophedon\n\ntransform of Millar, Sloane and Young.\n\n1 Introduction\n\nLet S n be the symmetric group on n elements. For a permutation π=π 1, . . . , π n+1 in the symmetric\n\ngroup on n+ 1 elements, S n+1, deﬁne the descent set of π to be the set\n\ndes(π) = {i:π i> π i+1}.\n\nObserve that des(π) is a subset of {1, . . . , n}.\n\nDeﬁnition 1.1 For a subset S of {1, . . . , n}, deﬁne the descent statistic β(S)to be the number of\n\npermutations in the symmetric group S n+1 that have descent set S.\n\nA central problem is to study how diﬀerent subsets compare on the basis of the descent statistic.\n\nThe approach that we take in this paper is to try and understand this statistic by restricting to certain\n\nfamilies of subsets.\n\nThe problem of maximizing β(S), where S ranges over all subsets of the set {1, . . . , n}, is answered\n\nby the following result:\n\nTheorem 1.2 (Niven, de Bruijn) The two subsets of {1, . . . , n}that maximize the descent statistic\n\nβ(S)are\n\n{1,3,5, . . .}∩{1, . . . , n}and {2,4,6, . . .}∩{1, . . . , n}.\n\n∗This paper appeared in Annals of Combinatorics 2(1998), 111–129.\n\n1\n\nThis theorem was independently proved by Niven [9] and de Bruijn [2]. The connection between this\n\nproblem and the cd-index was pointed out by Stanley and can be found in [10]. A short and elegant\n\nproof of this theorem using the cd-index appears in [4]. Another proof was given by Viennot [14]. In\n\nthis paper, we are generalizing the method used by Viennot.\n\nSagan, Yeh and Ziegler [11] considered the problem of maximizing the M¨obius function over cer-\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMaximizing the descent statistic - MaRDI portal (https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/wiki/Maximizing_the_descent_statistic)\nciteturn10search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 27.1 years ago; Crawled: last month; # Maximizing the descent statistic ... Authors Richard Ehrenborg, Swapneel Mahajan\n# Maximizing the descent statistic\n\nFrom MaRDI portal\n\nJump to:navigation, search\n\nDOI10.1007/BF01608482MaRDI QIDQ1305195zbMATH OpenFDO\n\nAuthors Richard Ehrenborg, Swapneel Mahajan\n\nPublication date 30 September 1999\n\nPublished in Annals of Combinatorics (Search for Journal in Brave)\n\nFull work available at URL https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01608482\n\nzbMATH Keywords\n\nboustrophedon transformdescent statisticEuler numberspermutations\n\nMathematics Subject Classification ID\n\nPermutations, words, matrices (05A05) Exact enumeration problems, generating functi